# What must NOT reach the build context, and the first group is the only one that matters.
#
# A .dockerignore is usually treated as an image-size optimisation. It is also a SECURITY
# boundary, and a stricter one than .gitignore: everything in the build context is sent to the
# daemon, and anything COPYed in survives in a layer even if a later step deletes it. `.env` is
# gitignored, so nothing stops a `COPY . .` from baking this project's API key into a
# distributable image - the one file most carefully kept out of git, shipped inside the
# artefact git was protecting.
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
keys.txt

# Build artefacts and measurement OUTPUT. The project's rule is commit inputs and code, never
# outputs; the same rule applies here, and the eval runs alone are several megabytes.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
chroma_db/
app.db
app.db-wal
app.db-shm
eval_*.jsonl
eval_*.txt
red_team*.jsonl
rewrite_*.jsonl
rt_*.jsonl
coverage_regression.jsonl
scope_methodology.jsonl
_mcp_server_stderr.log
_mcp_server_stderr_foreign.log
_to_delete/

# Throwaway diagnostics.
mcp_diag.py
mcp_diag_report.txt
_commitmsg*.txt

# History and CI config are not needed to RUN the thing.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# chroma_db is excluded above and that is deliberate, not an oversight: the index is BUILT by
# paid embedding calls and is mounted at runtime, never baked in. An image carrying the index
# would have to be rebuilt every time a filing is added, and it would ship 2,686 chunks of
# someone else's cost to anyone who pulls it.
